# Security model

Treat browser input, Graph data, AI output, Uniswap responses, RPC responses, webhooks, and persisted state as untrusted.

- Never expose secrets through `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`, client bundles, logs, stack traces, health endpoints, screenshots, or test fixtures.
- Never create an endpoint that signs caller-provided arbitrary calldata or arbitrary destinations.
- Verify authentication before associating a mandate with a treasury wallet.
- Verify the EIP-712 signature and every signed field server-side.
- Recheck live evidence, expiry, nonce, amount, token, router, recipient, slippage, and chain immediately before execution.
- Use a restrictive, non-empty Privy signer policy. No allow-all fallback.
- Enforce a global testnet amount cap independently of the user's mandate.
- Require idempotency. Never retry an uncertain broadcast automatically.
- Fail closed on stale evidence, schema drift, timeouts, malformed values, AI disagreement, or unknown routing types.
- Escape untrusted text. Do not use `dangerouslySetInnerHTML`.
- Use fixed allowlisted external origins; do not build user-controlled fetch URLs.
- Do not log full signatures, authorization material, request headers, or sensitive provider responses.
- Add a negative test with every new authorization rule.
- Document remaining trust assumptions in `docs/SECURITY_MODEL.md` and `LIMITATIONS.md`.

